src/models/dflash.cpp:138 - the one real build breakage of the b10481 bump. `ml->get_tensor_meta("d2t")` does not compile here.

MY FIRST ATTRIBUTION WAS WRONG AND IS RECORDED AS WRONG: I wrote that upstream b10481 is broken because it uses `ml->` inside a load_arch_tensors(llama_model_loader &) whose parameter it never names. Upstream master does not ship a file that cannot compile, and it does not here either - llama_model_base carries a member `llama_model_loader * ml` (src/llama-model.h line 767), so upstream's unnamed parameter is deliberate and `ml->` resolves to the MEMBER. The divergence is OURS: our signature names the parameter `ml`, which SHADOWS that member. Fixed to `ml.`, matching the sibling lookup twelve lines down that has always been `ml.`. Only occurrence in src/.

tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py, two defects found by RUNNING `bump` rather than reading it.
(1) cmd_bump read `args.ref if hasattr(args, "ref") else "master"` while bump's parser had NO --ref, so it ALWAYS took the hardcoded fallback - and `master` is a live branch here (9aa7b3d2b, the pre-recut history), so it printed a plausible rebase surface computed against a dead branch, with no error. sources.json's own _comment says lane-139 fixed "three argparse defaults"; this was the fourth. --ref added, fallback removed.
(2) NOT fixed, documented loudly in the runbook instead: bump's build and judge legs run against repo's own build-vulkan - the tree you are standing in, still on the OLD base - not against the replayed worktree. A green bump therefore never proves the new base builds. Moving the build into the throwaway worktree is a tool redesign, not a bump.

UPDATE-RUNBOOK.md - this is exactly what its old section 6 asked for: "the first real bump is the test of this document, and whoever runs it should fix this file where it is wrong rather than working around it".
- New 2.0: take the TAG, not the head, with the three tag-shaped facts that decide it.
- 2.1 rewritten with what `bump` actually does and does not prove.
- 2.2 gains the two things it did not warn about: a stale lane worktree holding arifi/main hostage so `git switch` refuses (that is 5.2 being violated, not a git quirk), and a lane working on its own branch needing --ref until merge-back. Plus the patches/ EOL churn (core.autocrlf=true against .gitattributes -text) that dirties the tree and makes `rebase --continue` refuse.
- New 6 documents the nightly drift watch.
- Old 6 becomes 7 and now tells the truth: 2 HAS been run once, 4.5 has not, 3 has never been run as a sweep.

licenses/README.md - the row d40e42fae's license file was missing (section 3 requires the row beside the retained license, so the retention was half-landed). The old thecodacus text is PRESERVED inside the correction section rather than deleted, because its error is the same one that section is about, one level out: every clause of "there is no thecodacus remote on this repository" was true, and the conclusion drawn from it was still wrong. NO LOCAL REMOTE IS NOT NO SOURCE.

