 ## 2. Upstream bump (the common case)
 
+### 2.0 Pick the target: the TAG, not the head
+
+Corrected 2026-08-18 by lane-152, the first live run. §2.2 below said `git log --oneline -5
+upstream/master`, which points you at the head. Take the newest **tag** at or below the head instead:
+
+```bash
+git fetch upstream
+git tag -l 'b10*' --sort=-v:refname | head -5
+git describe --tags upstream/master        # e.g. b10481-1-g8b8640097 -> 1 commit past b10481
+```
+
+Three things in this repository are tag-shaped and none of them is head-shaped: `sources.json` sets
+`track_tags: true` on upstream, it carries an `upstream_tag` field that must be filled with
+*something*, and the staged engine of record is named after the tag (`arifi-b10453-union`). A base
+that has no tag name forces every downstream artifact to be named after a bare sha. Take the head
+only when it carries engine content you actually need, and record *that* as the deviation.
+
 ### 2.1 One command, if it works
 
 ```bash
-python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py bump --onto <new-upstream-sha>
+python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py bump --onto <new-upstream-sha> --ref arifi/main
 ```
 
-`bump` runs replay + build + judge and **refuses unless all three pass**. When it passes, you are
-done except for §4.3 and the register flip.
+`bump` runs replay + build + judge and **refuses unless all three pass**.
+
+**Read this before you trust a green `bump`** (lane-152, measured on the first real execution):
+
+- **`--ref` is not optional in practice.** It was added by lane-152. Before that, `bump` had no
+  `--ref` at all and silently computed its rebase surface against a hardcoded `master`, which is a
+  dead pre-recut branch in this repository — a plausible-looking file list computed from the wrong
+  branch, with no error.
+- **Legs 3 and 4 do not test the bumped tree.** `bump` replays into a throwaway worktree
+  (`../.arifi-replay`), but `cmd_build` and `cmd_judge` operate on `repo`'s own `build-vulkan`, i.e.
+  the tree you are *standing in*, which is still on the OLD base. So a `bump` that reaches "all
+  three gates passed" has proven that the series replays AND that your current checkout still
+  builds — not that the new base builds. Until that is fixed, treat leg 2 (replay) as the real
+  gate and do §4.2/§4.3 yourself, on the rebased branch.
+- **Leg 4 refuses by default** without `--i-have-read-bench-purity`, so an unattended `bump` cannot
+  reach a pass at all.
+
+When leg 2 passes, go to §2.2's rebase; you have not saved yourself §4.
 
 ### 2.2 When `bump` refuses — the manual path
 
 `bump`'s failure names the patch, the file and the hunk. Do not guess at the rest.
 
 ```bash
-# 1. fetch upstream and pick the target
-git fetch upstream
-git log --oneline -5 upstream/master
+# 1. pick the target — §2.0, the tag, not the head
 
 # 2. dry-run the series onto the new base, in a scratch worktree, touching nothing
-python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py series replay --onto <new-upstream-sha>
+python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py series replay --onto <new-upstream-tag>
 ```
 
 If the replay is clean, move the branch and regenerate:
 
 ```bash
 git switch arifi/main
-git rebase --onto <new-upstream-sha> <old-base-sha> arifi/main
+git rebase --onto <new-upstream-tag> <old-base-sha> arifi/main
 #   ^ if this reports conflicts the replay lied; STOP and re-read the replay output.
 
 # update the base in sources.json: base.upstream_tag, base.upstream_sha  (base.ref stays "arifi/main")
@@ -78,6 +109,23 @@ python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py series regen
 python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py series check     # must exit 0
 ```
 
+Two things `git switch arifi/main` will do that this file did not warn about (lane-152):
+
+- **A stale lane worktree holds the branch hostage.** If any worktree still has `arifi/main` checked
+  out, `git switch` refuses with *"already used by worktree at …"*. That is §5.2 being violated, not
+  a git quirk — reap the worktree (`git worktree remove`), the branch is kept either way. Do a
+  `git worktree list` before you start; a lane that outlived its integration is the usual cause.
+- **A lane does this on its own branch, not on `arifi/main`.** Then `base.ref` in `sources.json`
+  still says `arifi/main` while the series describes the lane branch, so pass `--ref <lane-branch>`
+  to every `series regen|check|replay` until the merge-back makes the committed pair true again.
+
+**`rebase --continue` refuses while the tree is dirty, and this tree gets dirty on its own.** The
+repository sets `core.autocrlf=true` while `.gitattributes` marks `patches/**` as `-text`; the
+result is that several banked patch artifacts show up as whole-file modifications after a checkout,
+with insertions exactly equal to deletions. It is line-ending churn, not content — discard it
+(`git checkout -- patches/`) before each `--continue`. Do not commit it, and do not resolve it by
+hand: an equal-count whole-file diff on a generated artifact is never a real conflict.
+
 If the replay is **not** clean, resolve one patch at a time in the scratch worktree, and record why
 in the commit body. A resolution that is not explained is a resolution that will be re-broken.
 
